<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Pho</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wat Phra Chetuphon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Pho is a royal Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>. Rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> and greatly enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>, it houses a 46-metre gilded reclining Buddha and the largest collection of Buddha images in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Its inscriptions on medicine, history and literature made it a kind of public university, and it remains a centre of traditional Thai massage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-pho.jpg" alt="Wat Pho" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mastertongapollo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%98%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
